A||N||1||KRMFiero||Cradle Clean up?||||1||
Z||000000||KRMFiero||03-19-2001||03:32 PM||||Hey finnaly a nice day out! I took my engine cradle/tranny out of the garage and it is time to clean it, i bought degreeser and some glass beed for my sandblaster. What is the best ways to clean this up to get all the greese and oil and rust off it? The Tranny has all oil on it and stuff (eninge is not connected) is it okat for it to get wet? will the shaft or anything rust?  is there anything i should not get wet or anything?<P>Also how do you remove the cradle bushings?<P>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (Red)<BR>1985 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (White)<P>      <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/sig2.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by KRMFiero (edited 03-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||03-20-2001||10:35 AM||||<BR>I just had my cradle out of my car, and cleaned it up a little.<P>I would remove the tranny, tie rods, and rear bushings from the cradle, so you can man-handle, and flip it over and stuff like that for cleaning.<P>I just used a straight edge razor, parts brush, and carb cleaner to clean mine, took forever, but it is better - not so caked with grease.<P>The rear bushings lift up and out, the front have to be burned/drilled out (i believe).<P>Additionally, if you have the time, once you have it all cleaned up and rust free, put some protectant on the cradle to help prevent the rust from returning, and maybe paint it.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Down and Out for the Count)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
